A||N||13||Monkeyman||I HATE FEDEX!!!!!!!!||||8||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||10-23-2001||08:31 PM||||Stupid bastards!  I was supposed to get my wing today from them.  I called them yesterday to confirm this and it still says todays date on their website for delivery.  I had to remove my wing yesterday to send it to the gentleman who purchased it.  Now I've got 4 holes in my decklid.  I temporarily stuck some pieces of cloth in the holes today since it was supposed to sprinkle.  But...starting tonight, it's supposed to thunderstorm.  I tried a small piece of tape and when I removed it (after about 5 minutes), it pulled the paint off so that's out.  <P>Get this...My package left Chicago at 8:07 this morning.  Apparently, it has to go to Kokomo (about 4 hours from Chicago) before it can get delivered to me.  According to customer service it's not even supposed to arrive in Kokomo until 9:41 <B>tomorrow</B> night!!!  Can you believe that sh**?!?!  Are they walking it down there??????  I could have driven to Chicago after work today and picked the damn thing up and been back by now!!  The retarded customer service rep said that he <I>hoped</I> the package would arrive on my doorstep sometime Thursday, maybe.  WTF kind of service is this??  Pony Express?  How the hell do they stay in business?  One more reason I'll keep all of my business with UPS or USPS.  I may pay a few more bucks, but at least my parts will arrive when they're supposed to.<P>Yes, I'm pissed.  Yes, I'm rambling.  But this makes me feel just a tiny bit better.  (BTW--None of this reflects in any way on the sender.  He got it sent in a timely manner.)||||reg||8
Z||000001||Fiero5||10-23-2001||08:46 PM||||These businesses need more competition. I remember a few years ago when UPS went on strike, and screwed up thousands of businesses. Fed Ex isn't great, but I know many, many businesses that won't go back to UPS after that **** .<P>Steve||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||10-23-2001||08:57 PM||||I will <B>never</B> ship anything FedEx!  I don't care if UPS and the post office both go on strike.  I'd rather hand deliver something (or pay to have it hand delivered) rather than use FedEx.  I'm sorry, but they picked the wrong guy and the wrong night to piss me off.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  This is nothing but  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif">, plain and simple.  <B>It does not take 36 hours to drive 200 miles!!</B>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Back On Holiday||10-23-2001||10:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero5:<BR><B>These businesses need more competition. I remember a few years ago when UPS went on strike, and screwed up thousands of businesses. Fed Ex isn't great, but I know many, many businesses that won't go back to UPS after that **** .<P>Steve</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Do you KNOW why UPS went on strike?<P>------------------<BR>84se Silver 2m4<BR>86se Silver 2m6||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||10-23-2001||10:36 PM||||At one time or another, I have hated each of the parcel delivery services for a variety of reasons. The big thing that I like about FedEx is that they have normal Saturday delivery here where UPS does not. There is just something about getting new go-fast-goodies on Saturday morning  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||danny boy||10-23-2001||10:43 PM|||| You should have seen what FEDEX did to my otherwise perfect GT taillight I got from Pondski a while back. Still searching....||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wannabe||10-23-2001||11:30 PM||||Hate to say it, but time after time, Fedex has proven to be as good as it gets in the courrier business. I've had goods trashed and shipments lost or delayed by all of the major courriers. The one time it happened with Fedex they were very quick to make reparations. UPS on the other hand, operates in a constant state of confusion. Twice now I've had my account put on hold because they did not apply my payments. In one case, I gave them a check at pick up and they still sent me a bill (I stopped using them after that one). A few months later I get a collection note for the same invoice, even though they had cashed the check. to make it more fun, the credit department checks out at 4:30PM EST. Nuts.||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||10-24-2001||08:35 AM||||i'm glad i'm not the only one that gets screwed by FedEX... ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fiero5||10-24-2001||09:25 AM||||"Do you KNOW why UPS went on strike?"<P>Yup!<P>Listen, I am not saying that their reason for going on strike wasn't a valid one. I am only saying that when they did decide to go on strike, they left a very, very large customer base standing all alone that was relying on them to be there. They hurt a lot of businesses very badly. Why? Because UPS billed themselves as the best in town and are, and that you can count on them. UPS's customers are set up in such a way, that when UPS did a vanishing act, the businesses were left almost dead in the water. Yes, Fed Ex and other smaller shipping/delivery companies got all the business they could handle and then some. It's too bad they weren't able to use it more to their advantage. Why? To not only further their ability to grab a bigger piece of the pie, but to give UPS a stronger competitor and to help from this type of thing from ever effecting businesses in such a negative way again. It is only common sense to protect yourself though. I know that when this first happened, many businesses said that they were going to whatever it took to avoid getting screwed if UPS ever went on strike again. But now it has been quite awhile, and how soon they forget. I bet if UPS went on strike again, most of these businesses who said they would make sure that wasn't going to happen to them again, would say: "You know what, I let it happen to me again."  UPS is the biggest for now. But being the biggest doesn't always mean the best, and it also doesn't mean that their customers are protected if UPS decides to do something like go on strike and leave all their customers standing dead in the water again.<P>It's a catch 22 isn't it? UPS employees felt that they were getting the raw end of the stick, and wanted to strike, and did, to force the issue on what they wanted. Their is nothing wrong with that. The customers of UPS probably understood the situation that the UPS workers were fighting for, and understood their reasons why they did it. But it didn't help them any when they couldn't get the packages where they needed to go, and many because of it lost thousands (or more) and some even went under because of it. <BR>I think Fed Ex had a great opportunity to take advantage of that situation and jump up a few pegs on the package delivery business, but as much as anyone wants to complain about UPS, they are so big and so organized that for someone else to try and step up to that level in a short amount of time takes more than Fed Ex or anyone else apparently has.<P>I think that UPS has gotten so big, that many get the feeling that UPS has the attitude now that if you do not like us, go somewhere else knowing that they probably won't. Same attitude as Microsoft. When you get that big and that much market share, it can go to your head. Fed Ex being smaller usually will be a bit quicker to take care of any issues or problems, as they can't afford to upset the smaller customer base they get.<P>Some may say that this is all just my opinion, but having been in retail as long as I have, I speak from lot's of experience.<P>Steve||||reg||1
Z||000009||Philphine||10-24-2001||10:59 AM||||the main ups hub is in louisville. i don't know if fedex is the same but i've heard that no matter where you ship something, even across the street, every ups shipment comes here, gets sorted and goes back out again. maybe if fedex is the same it's taking longer than it would seem nesessary. just a guess.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Red2m4||10-24-2001||01:54 PM||||Actually, I think it is FedEx that does all their sorting in Louisville.  <P>It may seem like an asinine way of doing things, but I think the founder, Fred Smith, is a billionaire.  He came up with the idea for a college term paper, and got a "C".<P>I may not be able to fix my fiero, but I rule at trivial pursuit !||||reg||1
Z||000011||FieroRacer||10-24-2001||05:57 PM||||Please try to remember that with the recent anthrax scares in delivery services.  Shipments are going to take longer than usual.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Back On Holiday||10-24-2001||09:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero5:<BR><B>"Do you KNOW why UPS went on strike?"<P>Yup!<P>Listen, I am not saying that their reason for going on strike wasn't a valid one. I am only saying that when they did decide to go on strike, they left a very, very large customer base standing all alone that was relying on them to be there. They hurt a lot of businesses very badly. Why? Because UPS billed themselves as the best in town and are, and that you can count on them. UPS's customers are set up in such a way, that when UPS did a vanishing act, the businesses were left almost dead in the water. Yes, Fed Ex and other smaller shipping/delivery companies got all the business they could handle and then some. It's too bad they weren't able to use it more to their advantage. Why? To not only further their ability to grab a bigger piece of the pie, but to give UPS a stronger competitor and to help from this type of thing from ever effecting businesses in such a negative way again. It is only common sense to protect yourself though. I know that when this first happened, many businesses said that they were going to whatever it took to avoid getting screwed if UPS ever went on strike again. But now it has been quite awhile, and how soon they forget. I bet if UPS went on strike again, most of these businesses who said they would make sure that wasn't going to happen to them again, would say: "You know what, I let it happen to me again."  UPS is the biggest for now. But being the biggest doesn't always mean the best, and it also doesn't mean that their customers are protected if UPS decides to do something like go on strike and leave all their customers standing dead in the water again.<P>It's a catch 22 isn't it? UPS employees felt that they were getting the raw end of the stick, and wanted to strike, and did, to force the issue on what they wanted. Their is nothing wrong with that. The customers of UPS probably understood the situation that the UPS workers were fighting for, and understood their reasons why they did it. But it didn't help them any when they couldn't get the packages where they needed to go, and many because of it lost thousands (or more) and some even went under because of it. <BR>I think Fed Ex had a great opportunity to take advantage of that situation and jump up a few pegs on the package delivery business, but as much as anyone wants to complain about UPS, they are so big and so organized that for someone else to try and step up to that level in a short amount of time takes more than Fed Ex or anyone else apparently has.<P>I think that UPS has gotten so big, that many get the feeling that UPS has the attitude now that if you do not like us, go somewhere else knowing that they probably won't. Same attitude as Microsoft. When you get that big and that much market share, it can go to your head. Fed Ex being smaller usually will be a bit quicker to take care of any issues or problems, as they can't afford to upset the smaller customer base they get.<P>Some may say that this is all just my opinion, but having been in retail as long as I have, I speak from lot's of experience.<P>Steve</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>i'm going to answer this in sections...<P>Steve, Do you know WHY UPS went on strike?<BR>You answered the effect, not the cause.  Since your bad mouthing UPS I just thought I'd ask you if you knew the reasons why, if you don't - ok.<P>Strike?  the contract is 5 years, making the contract is up August 2002. - and This time They're working on it over a year ahead of time.<P>Fedex taking advantage of the situation?<BR>Yes they did, while UPS was getting back on their feet in Sept following the strike, FedEx Purchased RPS, UPS's closest competitor and now is called FedEx Ground.<P>it's Important that people realize the reasons for unions striking.<BR><P>------------------<BR>84se Silver 2m4<BR>86se Silver 2m6||||reg||1
Z||000013||Philphine||10-24-2001||11:48 PM||||nope ups is in louisville. right across from my guard base and swallowing everything in sight. our base moved across the airport to accomodate them. i think they even paid for part of it.||||reg||1
